title: Filesystem coordination
tags: [[Revert]]
kind: reference
summary: The daemon says which file action each operation of a revert needs; the client carries it out — move, restore from the trash, send back to it — or leaves the operation out.

The daemon applies metadata and does not judge whether an action is possible on disk:
that belongs to the client, which sees the files, the permissions and the user. For each
operation of a revert it says what undoing it would take — `filesystem.action` in the plan
([[Revert endpoints]]):

|!Action |!When |!The client |
|`move` |a `file_moved` |checks the file is at `from`, applies the move policy ([[Reverting a revision]]), runs the move |
|`restore_content` |undoing a `file_deleted`/`file_modified`, or bringing back a metarecord a trashing took |looks in the trash for this metarecord's content and moves it back — ''auto-restoring'' exactly what was deleted |
|`trash_content` |taking back the undo of a trashing (a redo) |sends the file back to the trash, in a new entry, before the revert deletes the metarecord |

The op types cannot tell the trash cases apart: a trashing's `delete_metarecord` is known
by its revision's `origin = trash`, but its undo is an ordinary-looking
`create_metarecord`, and the redo of that an ordinary deletion — the daemon follows their
`reverts_op_id` chain back to the trashing. A trash entry is found by ''the metarecord it
holds'', not by an id recorded on the operation: an undo consumes the entry and a redo
makes a new one, so a recorded id would name something gone. Only one live entry can hold
a metarecord. For `file_deleted`/`file_modified` the entry is matched by metarecord and
''the version before the revision'' (`entity_version_before_revision` — the first
operation's `entity_version_before` by `seq`, since versions are hashes and have no
order).

''Leaving an operation out is free.'' An action the client could not carry out — no trash
entry left, a file not where the move expects it under the `skip` policy — leaves that
operation out of the commit: it is not reverted, and nothing needs correcting afterwards
([[What a revert writes]]).

!! The lock

`revert/start` enters the ''coordinated-revert lock'':

* the watcher keeps running and buffering, but ''nothing is executed'' — so a move the client just made is not ingested as a new file before the metadata explaining it lands;
* every write endpoint answers `423 Locked`; reads work as usual;
* `revert/commit`, or `revert/abort`, releases it; then the held events are applied as a new revision, with the watcher's usual compaction and batching ([[Event batching]]).

The lock is not stored. A daemon that crashes restarts unlocked, with nothing of the
revert written; the buffered watcher events are lost with everything else that happened
while it was down — a reconcile is what recovers that. A move done before the crash
leaves `mfr_path` stale until a reconcile corrects it.

The execution — policies, trash lookups, retrashing — is `core::navigation`, shared by the
CLI and the GUI (which never asks questions under the lock: it applies moves, and applies
the metadata when the file is gone).
